{"id":"h299","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"The maximum weekly dose for the hands and forearms, as-suming a mass of 6kg and a surface area of 950 cm^2, is 2.0 rem. How long would it take for a person to receive this dose from a 15millicuriesource emitting a 3.0MeVgamma ray? The distance between the source and the receiving surface is 15 cm. Assume no radiation is absorbed by the air.\nIs the correct answer \"22.6 min\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"no","correct":false,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct answer is not 22.6 min.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1409},{"text":"Treating the source as a point emitter with one 3.0 MeV photon per decay is a natural assumption.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1399},{"text":"All photon energy reaching the hands is absorbed, with quality factor 1 and no air attenuation.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1403},{"text":"The activity of 15 mCi is 5.55×10^8 decays per second.","confidence":0.95,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1406},{"text":"The dose rate to the hands is about 1.49×10^-3 rem/s.","confidence":0.85,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1405},{"text":"The time to reach 2.0 rem is about 22.3 min under the stated assumptions.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":3.1408},{"text":"The 1-2% difference from 22.6 min could come from rounding constants or from how the absorbed fraction is modeled.","confidence":0.4,"lean":1,"theta":0.6283,"phi":3.1401},{"text":"Reaching 22.6 min would require a dose rate about 1% lower, which the stated data do not support.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-1,"theta":1.2566,"phi":3.1409}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":4,"J":0.4,"relation":"supp